7592186111095 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533954 true] [17592186111095 1145 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962359/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)" 13194139533954 true] [17592186111283 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533954 true] [17592186111283 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268418 1017 #edb/ref 17592186268419 13194139533954 true] [17592186268418 1089 "q-116942" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268418 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533954 true] [17592186268418 1100 "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268418 1113 #uuid "ceebcce2-da76-556e-a74b-03eb33df1596" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268418 1145 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962359/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268420 13194139533954 true] [17592186268419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268421 13194139533954 true] [17592186268419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268422 13194139533954 true] [17592186268419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268423 13194139533954 true] [17592186268419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268424 13194139533954 true] [17592186268419 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533954 true] [17592186268419 1041 