lowing:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268469 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268470 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268471 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268472 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268473 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1041 "selection" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1104 #edb/ref 17592186268473 13194139533954 true] [17592186268468 1110 #uuid "c9c3fc60-5205-5123-b6b5-26335e1990c0" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268469 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268469 1103 #edb/ref 1759218604