8493 1034 "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533954 true] [17592186268493 1111 #uuid "8c3ebc08-b9a7-56dd-bb02-b3c4b533d519" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268494 1034 "(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533954 true] [17592186268494 1111 #uuid "f050a153-b211-59f7-bc2f-40e849dde683" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268495 1017 #edb/ref 17592186268496 13194139533954 true] [17592186268495 1089 "q-65821" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268495 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533954 true] [17592186268495 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(0,-2\\pi )$\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268495 1113 #uuid "23074372-05ee-554d-b915-2199d9c252b0" 13194139533954 true] [17592186268495 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorn